<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishak Bey of Saruhan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1388">1388</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ishak Bey was ruler of the Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> of Saruhan in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a>, in the second half of the 14th century. He was the son of the dynasty's founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruhan-bey">Saruhan Bey</a>. His reign saw the beylik prosper from the fertile Gediz plain and trade with the Genoese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a>, and in 1366–1367 he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-manisa">Great Mosque of Manisa</a>, which still stands. Saruhan was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> soon after his death.</p></div>
